This comment is intended for Question 36.[^1]

The Commission should not treat an electronic address supplied by an affiliate or third party as, by itself, an address the covered recipient has “provided” or “accepted to use” for another covered entity’s securities-law disclosures. An electronic address may be accurate and lawfully shared without the recipient knowing that a different entity will use it to replace paper delivery. We support the distinction reflected in the proposing release’s discussion of third-party-supplied addresses.
The Commission should distinguish a service provider delivering information on behalf of the same covered entity from a separate covered entity seeking to satisfy its own delivery obligation. For the latter case, any exception should require the receiving entity to document the address’s source and its current association with the recipient, listing the categories of information and delivery methods used with clear notice through an established channel, and obtain a recipient action confirming that the identified address may be used for that entity’s covered information before discontinuing paper. Other communication that does not pass that set bar such as a test message, absence of a bounce-back, general privacy notice, or broad terms-of-service amendment should not alone establish that choice. A prior affirmative paper election should remain effective unless the recipient changes it with intention and of clear record. Any removal of specific verification of the recipient involved could introduce more distance between the managing entity and the investor or limit the ability of an owner to make informed and current decisions on their directly held investments. 
The Commission should require records of address provenance, the notice, the recipient’s confirmation, delivery failures, remediation, and any paper election. The recipient should be able to correct the address and obtain or elect paper delivery without charge. A known failed delivery should trigger timely delivery of the affected information through a functioning address or paper, consistent with proposed section 303.102(h). For materials tied to voting, tendering, or another deadline, the Commission should consider whether a second channel or a more specific confirmation is needed before a newly supplied address replaces an established channel. 
Finally, the Commission should address the practical value of paper choice and confirm it in new language. Although the proposal already provides paper without a delivery fee, the release states that a covered entity may, where otherwise lawful and disclosed, restrict or terminate a relationship after a paper election. The Commission should evaluate whether that consequence undermines a meaningful opt-out and should prohibit making an investor’s continued access to ordinary services conditional on accepting electronic delivery, subject to any narrowly justified exceptions, and 303.102 could be amended to more clearly reflect that[^2]. We do not ask the Commission to ban legitimate operational sharing of contact information; we ask it not to equate that sharing with proof that legally required information was effectively delivered. The exception for paper delivery discontinuation should apply only when the receiving entity can demonstrate that the recipient was informed through a reliable channel and had an effective, documented means to preserve paper delivery[^3]. Investors must be able to retain complete trust and confidence that material information will reliably be delivered to them. 
There must be a record of investors being provided an opportunity to review the information and to opt out, and covered entities must utilize an established channel for communication for a given investor. This careful approach will take additional time but it will lay a strong foundation for the industry to move forward and scale adequately. What must be avoided is the possibility that a large amount of investors are lost in this transition - covered entities must be diligent in tracking their outreach, and in maintaining a record of that outreach. That record should maintained by the covered entity, be provided to regulators on request and should be available to account holders for general inspection. 

